From 451be9a6302ffee94bb958945dd3d4e3c22dada6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 10:52:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/14] util/u_debug: decide __normal_user from the C library,
 not from the OS
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

__normal_user() answers "did this process gain privileges its invoker
did not have", and everything that trusts a filename from the
environment consults it: the log file, the trace file, the disk cache,
several shader-override paths. Its implementation compares the real and
effective ids, guarded by `#if defined(_WIN32)` — an OS name standing in
for the property that actually matters, which is whether the C library
has the set-user-ID concept at all.

A C library that does not have it cannot have raised the process's
privileges, so `true` is the correct answer for that whole class, and it
is the answer Windows already gets. Testing HAVE_GETEUID says so
directly: Windows keeps `true` because it has no geteuid, every POSIX
platform keeps the comparison because it has one, and a freestanding
libc stops failing to link over a question it cannot be asked.

POSIX.1 groups geteuid/getuid/getegid/getgid together under process
identification, and no C library implements a subset, so one check
covers the expression.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 4 item 3 (memory objects — the libc gaps the
  first executable link meets)
Why: devkitA64's newlib declares all four in <unistd.h> and defines none
  of them. Horizon has no users, no set-user-ID bit and no way for a
  homebrew process to acquire privileges, so the question has no
  meaning here; the code still has to link, because util/log.c reaches
  __normal_user() while initialising the logger, and every Vulkan driver
  logs.
Evidence: link probes against switch.specs, one translation unit each:
    geteuid  FAILS: undefined reference to `geteuid'
    getuid   FAILS: undefined reference to `getuid'
    getgid   FAILS: undefined reference to `getgid'
    getegid  FAILS: undefined reference to `getegid'
  and none of the four is a compiler builtin, so cc.has_function()
  answers NO honestly with or without a prefix (measured from Meson
  1.11.2's own template, all four -> NO). An nm audit of Mesa's built
  non-driver core — 10 archives, every undefined symbol resolved against
  the core itself and then against the whole toolchain — leaves six
  unresolved, four of which are these, referenced from log.c.o and
  perf_u_trace.c.o. log.c.o is reachable: nir_print.c.o references it.
Upstream: yes — it replaces an OS test with the trait that test was
  standing in for, changes no behaviour on any platform that has the
  function, and keeps Windows on the branch it is already on.
---
 meson.build        |  1 +
 src/util/u_debug.h | 11 ++++++++---
 2 files changed, 9 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/meson.build b/meson.build
index 858c665..3347046 100644
--- a/meson.build
+++ b/meson.build
@@ -1573,6 +1573,7 @@ functions_to_detect = {
   'posix_fallocate': '',
   'secure_getenv': '',
   'sysconf': '#include <unistd.h>',
+  'geteuid': '#include <unistd.h>',
 }
 
 foreach f, prefix: functions_to_detect
diff --git a/src/util/u_debug.h b/src/util/u_debug.h
index 9608e97..f74612e 100644
--- a/src/util/u_debug.h
+++ b/src/util/u_debug.h
@@ -402,10 +402,15 @@ debug_get_option_ ## suffix (void) \
 static inline bool
 __normal_user(void)
 {
-#if defined(_WIN32)
-   return true;
-#else
+#ifdef HAVE_GETEUID
    return geteuid() == getuid() && getegid() == getgid();
+#else
+   /* A C library with no set-user-ID concept cannot have raised this
+    * process's privileges, so there is nothing here to distrust.
+    * Windows is the platform this branch was written for and it keeps
+    * the same answer.
+    */
+   return true;
 #endif
 }
 
